      "title": "epan: BCD strings with multiple filler nibbles at the end",
      "message": "epan: BCD strings with multiple filler nibbles at the end\n\n3GPP TS 29.060 specifies (7.7.2) that the IMSI IE always takes\n8 octets, even though the IMSI can be smaller. Unused semi-octets\nare always 1 1 1 1 (0xf). This means that when there is a 14\ndigit IMSI the last octet is 0xff.\n\nThe current tvb_get_bcd_string() assumes that the stop digit only\noccurs in the high nibble (reversed for big endian), i.e. that it\nis only used when there are an odd number of nibbles. Treat\n0xf as a stop digit whether it occurs in either nibble.\n\nAlso fix the treatment of the stop digit in the Big Endian case,\nwhere it was not being properly masked.\n\nFix #19716\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
      "author_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
